#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:131
msgid "Differences in User Interfaces"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:133
msgid "No Indexing"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:135
msgid ""
"If you've used PyCharm on large projects, you know the wait: \"Indexing...\" "
"can take anywhere from 30 seconds to several minutes depending on project "
"size and dependencies. PyCharm builds a comprehensive index of your entire "
"codebase to power its code intelligence, and it re-indexes when dependencies "
"change or when you install new packages."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:137
msgid ""
"Zed doesn't index. You open a folder and start working immediately. File "
"search and navigation stay fast regardless of project size, without waiting "
"through indexing pauses."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:139
msgid ""
"PyCharm's index powers features like finding all usages across your entire "
"codebase, understanding class hierarchies, and detecting unused imports "
"project-wide. Zed delegates this work to language servers, which may not "
"analyze as deeply or as broadly."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:141 src/migrate/pycharm.md:168
#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:186 src/migrate/pycharm.md:208
#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:239
msgid "**How to adapt:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:143
msgid ""
"For project-wide symbol search, use `Cmd+O` / Go to Symbol (relies on your "
"language server)"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:144
msgid "For finding files by name, use `Cmd+Shift+O` / Go to File"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:145
msgid ""
"For text search across files, use `Cmd+Shift+F`—this is fast even on large "
"codebases"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:146
msgid ""
"For deep static analysis, consider running tools like `mypy`, `pylint`, or "
"`ruff check` from the terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:148
msgid "LSP vs. Native Language Intelligence"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:150
msgid ""
"PyCharm has its own language analysis engine built specifically for Python. "
"This engine understands your code deeply: it resolves types without "
"annotations, tracks data flow, knows about Django models and Flask routes, "
"and offers specialized refactorings."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:152
msgid ""
"Zed uses the Language Server Protocol (LSP) for code intelligence. For "
"Python, Zed provides several language servers out of the box:"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:154
msgid "**basedpyright** (default) — Fast type checking and completions"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:155
msgid "**Ruff** (default) — Linting and formatting"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:156
msgid "**ty** — Up-and-coming language server from Astral, built for speed"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:157
msgid "**Pyright** — Microsoft's type checker"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:158
msgid "**PyLSP** — Plugin-based server with tool integrations"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:160
msgid ""
"The LSP experience for Python is strong. basedpyright provides accurate "
"completions, type checking, and navigation. Ruff handles formatting and "
"linting with excellent performance."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:162
msgid "Where you might notice differences:"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:164
msgid ""
"Framework-specific intelligence (Django ORM, Flask routes) isn't built-in"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:165
msgid ""
"Some complex refactorings (extract method with proper scope analysis) may be "
"less sophisticated"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:166
msgid ""
"Auto-import suggestions depend on what the language server knows about your "
"environment"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:170
msgid ""
"Use `Alt+Enter` for available code actions—the list will vary by language "
"server"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:171
msgid ""
"Ensure your virtual environment is selected so the language server can "
"resolve your dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:172
msgid "Use Ruff for fast, consistent formatting (it's enabled by default)"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:173
msgid ""
"For code inspection similar to PyCharm's \"Inspect Code,\" run `ruff check ."
"` or check the Diagnostics panel (`Cmd+6`)—basedpyright and Ruff together "
"catch many of the same issues"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:175
msgid "Virtual Environments and Interpreters"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:177
msgid ""
"In PyCharm, you select a Python interpreter through a GUI, and PyCharm "
"manages the connection between your project and that interpreter. It shows "
"available packages, lets you install new ones, and keeps track of which "
"environment each project uses."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:179
msgid "Zed handles virtual environments through its toolchain system:"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:181
msgid ""
"Zed automatically discovers virtual environments in common locations (`."
"venv`, `venv`, `.env`, `env`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:182
msgid "When a virtual environment is detected, the terminal auto-activates it"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:183
msgid ""
"Language servers are automatically configured to use the discovered "
"environment"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:184
msgid ""
"You can manually select a toolchain if auto-detection picks the wrong one"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:188
msgid ""
"Create your virtual environment with `python -m venv .venv` or `uv sync`"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:189
msgid "Open the folder in Zed—it will detect the environment automatically"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:190
msgid "If you need to switch environments, use the toolchain selector"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:191
msgid ""
"For conda environments, ensure they're activated in your shell before "
"launching Zed"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:193
msgid ""
"**Tip:** If basedpyright shows import errors for packages you've installed, "
"check that Zed has selected the correct virtual environment. Use the "
"toolchain selector to verify or change the active environment."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:195
msgid "No Project Model"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:197
msgid ""
"PyCharm manages projects through `.idea` folders containing XML "
"configuration files, interpreter assignments, and run configurations. This "
"model lets PyCharm remember your interpreter choice, manage dependencies "
"through the UI, and persist complex run/debug setups."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:199
msgid ""
"Zed has no project model. A project is a folder. There's no wizard, no "
"interpreter selection screen, no project structure configuration."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:201
msgid "This means:"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:203
msgid ""
"Run configurations don't exist. You define tasks or use the terminal. Your "
"existing PyCharm run configs in `.idea/` won't be read—you'll recreate the "
"ones you need in `tasks.json`."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:204
msgid ""
"Interpreter management is external. Zed discovers environments but doesn't "
"create them."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:205
msgid ""
"Dependencies are managed through pip, uv, poetry, or conda—not through the "
"editor."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:206
msgid ""
"There's no Python Console (interactive REPL) panel. Use `python` or "
"`ipython` in the terminal instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:210
msgid ""
"Create a `.zed/settings.json` in your project root for project-specific "
"settings"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/pycharm.md:211
msgid ""
"Define common commands in `tasks.json` (open via Command Palette: `zed: open "
"tasks`):"
msgstr ""

